 "autor": "claude",
 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Why is almost every manhole cover on Earth round?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It's not for looks. It's a safety trick.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Under the cover, a shaft drops down to the pipes, sometimes ten meters deep.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The lid rests on a small lip, just a bit narrower than the lid itself.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A square lid has a problem. Turn it on its edge, and it slips through its own hole.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The diagonal of a square is longer than its side, so it fits down the shaft.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A circle is the same width in every direction.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "No matter how you tilt it, it can't fall in.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That matters, because a cast iron lid can weigh as much as a grown man.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Round lids don't need lining up, either. Drop one in any way, and it fits.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Workers can roll it on its edge instead of carrying it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And a round shaft is stronger, because the soil presses evenly all around it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Some cities do use square or triangle covers, but they need a deep lip or a hinge.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Big tech companies once asked this in job interviews, to see how people think.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So next time you step on one, you'll know why it's round.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
